+# Deferred-reader cost model and later-reading prerequisites
+
+This is the residual acceptance and experiment design for [#447](https://github.com/buster14a/buster/issues/447),
+and the caller prerequisite for **item 3 only** of [#697](https://github.com/buster14a/buster/issues/697).
+It audits main `e7f89091648f68940b595aadd648841c1ffc3ec5`. It does not change
+the lane rule, the first-reading copy, the merge, or the source-order driver.
+#697's existing owner retains items 1 and 2, including the cold-rule refit;
+item 4 subsequently merged as #719 at `16d5c61704eae82a0c40607771b5f3dc0f207d97`.
+That later merge is outside this frozen experiment.
+
+## What the existing evidence establishes
+
+The original issue's acceptance is wider than the rule implemented by #448.
+#683 subsequently changed the rule and the lanes' reservation/scheduling costs.
+Use the [deferred-reader guide](c-deferred-bodies.md) and its
+[frozen A2/B2/CT2 record](https://github.com/buster14a/buster-performance/tree/e4ae6019ed75575d49f9e75fba752820a1da9050/docs/measurements/deferred-lane-costs-113-2026-10-27)
+for those historical results. They do not measure current main.
+
+| #447 requirement | Available evidence | Remaining gate |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| Low-overhead work-based lane admission | Current rule uses total/largest/second-largest body tokens and top-level tokens; #448 and #683 include same-binary forced-lane controls. | A weighted model needs independent evidence that collection and prediction earn their cost. |
+| Source order, deferred 1/2/3/4/8, adaptive on the same unit | A2 has forced 2/3/4/8 against 1 and source order, with the parse/phases apart. B2 compares each rule on permitted 8 against 1. | This is not one fully crossed adaptive grid at each permitted count on current main; warm A2 has only forced 2 and 8. |
+| Tiny, uneven and many bodies | Four c-testsuite units, SQLite and 38 synthetic units in A2/B2; CT2 covers 220 programs. | The synthetic bodies have one statement shape; a per-kind fit cannot treat their repetitions as independent grammar diversity. |
+| A real libc workload on the 9700X | The guide records laptop differential checks of musl source: 1,486 units merged, 38 left to source order, zero differences. | This is correctness evidence. Headers included by synthetic units do not satisfy the requested real-libc timing workload. No such workload is in the 43-unit A2/B2 grid. |
+| Absolute reader times | Frozen headers record tokens and rounded ns/token; B2 has five rounds with input identities and commands. | Multiplying the printed value by tokens derives an approximate absolute median. Its rounding bound is ±0.005 ns × tokens, before any further summary rounding. It is not a retained unrounded absolute sample or whole-process compile time. |
+| Peak RSS | The historical record explicitly disclaims a resident-memory series. | Measure process peak and retained-state RSS separately, including different-unit reuse. Reserved virtual capacity does not establish resident memory. |
+| Types, bindings, token columns, graph, diagnostics and later results; forced/random schedules and fallback | The guide's deferred fixtures, corpus comparison, poisoned/kept-state rereads, forced lanes, delayed first lane and failure-after-replay tests cover these contracts. | A future policy change must rerun exact-head differential and sanitizer gates. Old functional results are not new-main qualification. |
+| No regression against the best fixed count | Historical A2/B2 compare the reader against its own one-lane control and report spread/code-placement limits. | Refit after the active first-read-copy change. A better body-time prediction alone does not establish a better whole-reader lane decision. |
+
+Cold in these records means a fresh deferred state and first touches of its
+copies' pages. It does not mean a cold CPU cache, cold filesystem cache, or a
+new compiler process. The one-lane path still does deferred setup and merge;
+choosing one lane is not an unsupported-semantic fallback to source order.
+
+## Offline weighted-work experiment
+
+The current prefix costs are body token counts. A weighted proposal assigns
+nonnegative costs to parser kinds and sums them over each body's bounded
+opening-to-closing range. That is a hypothesis about the **body walk**, not
+the top-level walk, position restoration, reader copies, merge, later phases,
+or complete compilation.
+
+There are **83 parser kinds** at this source pin. A 64-byte `vpermb` table
+would alias kinds above 63. An eventual byte-weight implementation needs an
+exact 128-entry lookup (for example `vpermi2b`), or a separately proved kind
+mapping, plus a bounded scalar sum as its independent oracle. No table or
+extra production token pass is introduced here.
+
+The existing benchmark times each body's walk internally but publishes their
+sum. It does not already provide per-body training rows. An offline adapter
+may expose those existing timestamps, collect per-kind histograms **outside**
+the timed reading, and retain the raw body samples and exact adapter source.
+Its results belong to the instrumented adapter, not the release compiler.
+
+The token baseline and per-kind fit must share the same nonnegative fixed
+per-body term. Start the per-kind problem at the token fit: equal kind
+weights are a feasible token-count model. A lower training error follows
+from extra degrees of freedom and is not evidence of better held-out
+prediction. Split by complete translation unit and corpus; never put repeated
+bodies from one generated unit into both sets. Record absent kinds and
+collinear counts. Do not mistake one chosen fit of those coefficients for
+uniquely identified checking costs.
+
+The first bounded local experiment was rejected: a six-unit, 124-body fit
+reduced training RMSE from 2,133 to 961 ns, but increased held-out RMSE from
+132,378 to 1,853,104 ns on four other c-testsuite units, from 16,427 to
+64,339 ns on SQLite, and from 4,525 to 11,574 ns on five musl source units.
+These are instrumented local Zen 4 body-walk prediction errors, not reader
+speedups or 9700X results. No weights, policy or production caller were
+introduced, and no tuning or additional training data was sought. The acceptance gates
+below remain open.
+
+Before production consideration:
+
+1. Fit on independently varied bodies, report the token and weighted models
+   on held-out c-testsuite, SQLite, libc and repository units, and retain
+   negative results. Check integer byte-weight quantization separately.
+2. On the locked 9700X, isolate the bounded histogram/weighted-sum overhead
+   and compare whole-reader source order, forced 1/2/3/4/8 and adaptive
+   choices on the same source and inputs. Include absolute time and RSS.
+3. If weighted costs also choose parts, consistently select the largest
+   **weighted** body and its critical path. Reusing the largest-token body
+   would invalidate the first-lane placement assumption. Preserve stable
+   ties, lazy reader construction, `parts_by_cost` and the forced controls.
+4. Coordinate after #697's owned first-read-copy work and cold refit; do not
+   silently refit or replace that policy from this experiment.
+
+## Later-reading readiness: item 3 of #697
+
+No production caller of `check_input_deferred` exists at this source pin.
+The driver uses source order. Its callers are the corpus/fixture checks and
+the benchmark. The corpus's reread control and poisoned-memory fixtures are
+test paths. `benchmark-checker-deferred --warm` retains one state while
+reading the **same unit**, alternating one and many lanes; it releases that
+state at the end of the file. It does not demonstrate a production caller
+reading different units with one state.
+
+Consequently, the 256-token warm-grid observation remains a frozen offline
+candidate. There is no production warm rule to enable or claim as complete.
+Do not invent a multi-unit caller solely to make that rule reachable.
+
+A genuine caller must first establish:
+
+- **Lifetime and reset:** one reading at a time, with all prior `CheckInput`
+  users finished before the next reading reclaims state memory; each unit
+  supplies the required fresh caller type table and current parser,
+  preprocessor and identifier context. Retained pages do not authorize
+  retaining old semantic contents.
+- **Per-lane coverage:** a retained state pointer or a nonzero reading count
+  does not prove readiness. A previous one-lane unit leaves other lanes
+  untouched; a later larger top level may touch pages the lane never used.
+  Readiness must describe the lane and sufficient touched backing for the
+  current unit, without treating virtual capacity or copied arena high-water
+  metadata as proof. Missing evidence must take the conservative policy.
+- **Invalidation:** release/re-reservation, allocation failure, a change of
+  storage representation and partially prepared/fallback readings must have
+  explicit effects on readiness. Allocation success alone is not a complete
+  warm-state signal. Any metadata must follow the actual owner of pages.
+- **Admission and reporting:** keep permitted, selected and actually active
+  lanes, copies, memory readiness, and semantic fallback reasons distinct.
+  A warmed benchmark flag is an experiment control, not a production oracle.
+- **Qualification:** test small→large and large→small units, changed names
+  and types, 1→8→1 lanes, lanes first used on a later unit, fresh/reused/dirty
+  and borrowed arenas, resource rollback, unsupported units and release.
+  Compare every result with independent source order and scalar paths, then
+  measure first and later readings separately on current native code.
+
+The prerequisite remains open. Reassess these contracts when a real caller
+such as #152 exists and after the active copy change establishes its new
+storage/readiness contract. A speculative process-wide `warm` boolean would
+not meet it.
+
+This document and the offline experiment are original project work. External
+corpora are validation inputs with pinned provenance, not imported compiler
+implementation. No new compiler dependency is required.
